          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong linearly distributive category
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+structures"} monoidal structures
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            (“times”) and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊕
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus
            (“par” written as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⅋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \invamp
            in linear logic) that “distribute” in the sense that there is a map (not necessarily invertible)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\delta \colon A \otimes (B \oplus C) \longrightarrow (A \otimes B) \oplus C
          %p
            Linearly distributive categories are the “representable” form of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polycategories"} polycategories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"} star-autonomous category
            when the involution is forgotten but the dual tensor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⅋
                  %mi B
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\invamp B \coloneqq (A^* \otimes B^*)^*
            is remembered.
          %p
            Another way to say essentially the same thing is that a
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
            in which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit"} counit
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+objects"} dual objects
            refer to two different tensor products: we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top \to A \oplus A^*
            but
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^* \otimes A \to \bot
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\otimes,\top)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\oplus,\bot)
            are two different monoidal structures. A linearly distributive category encodes the necessary relationship between two such monoidal structures such that this makes sense, i.e. such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangle+identities"} triangle identities
            can be stated.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong linearly distributive category
            (also called a
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong weakly distributive category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            in two ways, with monoidal structures
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\otimes, \top)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\oplus, \bot)
            equipped with extra associator natural transformations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi δ
                    %mi L
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta^L:A \otimes (B\oplus C) \to (A \otimes B) \oplus C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi δ
                    %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta^R:(A \oplus B) \otimes C \to A \oplus (B \otimes C)
          %p
            and the obvious six pentagon equations and four triangle equations that make the monoidal structures work together nicely (
            %a{:href => "#CockettSeely97"}> Cockett-Seely 1997
            ). (Some of these equations can be interpreted as saying that the
            = succeed "s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensorial+strengths"} tensorial strengths
            for the functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A\oplus -)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-\oplus C)
            with respect to the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            monoidal structure.) A
            %strong symmetric linearly distributive category
            is one in which both monoidal structures are symmetric and three squares commute relating the symmetries to the
            = succeed "s." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
          %p
            These extra associators are sometimes called “distributors”, and should not be confused with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
          %p
            In a symmetric LDC, the symmetry maps induce the following
            %strong permuting
            distributivity maps:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi δ
                    %mi R
                    %mi L
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta^L_R : A \otimes (B \oplus C) \to B \oplus (A \otimes C)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi δ
                    %mi L
                    %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta^R_L : (B \oplus C) \otimes A \to (B \otimes A) \oplus C
          %p
            An LDC which is equipped with all the four linear distributivity maps is said to be
            = succeed "." do
              %strong non-planar
          %p
            %strong Warning:
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+category"} distributive category
            %em cannot
            be made into a linearly distributive category with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo =
                  %mo ×
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes = \times
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus = +
            unless it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partial order
            (but every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive lattice
            is indeed a linearly distributive category) (for a proof, see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#CockettSeely97"} Cockett-Seely 1997
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"} star-autonomous category
                is linearly distributive, with
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊕
                        %mi B
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mo *
                        %mo ⊗
                        %msup
                          %mi B
                          %mo *
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \oplus B = (A^* \otimes B^*)^*
            %li
              %p
                Any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive lattice
                is linearly distributive, with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi B
                      %mo =
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes  B = A\wedge B
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊕
                        %mi B
                        %mo =
                        %mi A
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\oplus B = A\vee B
                %em not
                extend to
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+categories"} distributive categories
            %li
              %p
                Any monoidal category is linearly distributive trivially with
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊕
                        %mo =
                        %mo ⊗
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus = \otimes
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invertible+object"} invertible object
                in a monoidal category, then defining
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mi B
                      %mo =
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi D
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\oplus B = A\otimes D \otimes B
                yields a linearly distributive structure whose linear distributors are isomorphisms.
            %li
              %p
                The category of modules for a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebra"} bialgebra
                in any linearly distributive category (hence, in particular, in any
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"} star-autonomous category
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
          %h2#linear_functors_and_transformations Linear functors and transformations
          %p
            A
            %strong linear functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:C\to D
            between linearly distributive categories consists of a pair of functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊕
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\otimes, F_\oplus
            such that,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\otimes
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+functor"} lax monoidal functor
            with respect to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊕
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\oplus
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colax+monoidal+functor"} colax monoidal functor
            with respect to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊕
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\otimes (A\oplus B) \to F_\oplus(A) \oplus F_\otimes (B)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\otimes (A\oplus B) \to F_\otimes(A) \oplus F_\oplus(B)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\otimes (A) \otimes F_\oplus(B) \to F_\oplus(A\otimes B)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\oplus (A) \otimes F_\otimes(B) \to F_\oplus(A\otimes B)
          %p
            satisfying appropriate coherence conditions (
            %a{:href => "#CockettSeely99"}> Cockett-Seely 1999
            ). A
            %strong linear natural transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha: F\to G
            consists of a monoidal transformations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_\otimes: F_\otimes \to G_\otimes
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mo ⊕
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mo ⊕
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊕
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_\oplus: G_\oplus \to F_\oplus
            satisfying further coherence conditions. This defines a 2-category of linearly distributive categories, which has the property of embedding the 2-category of
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
          %p
            On the other hand, a linear functor in this sense is more general than a functor between underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polycategories"} polycategories
            (see below). The latter probably coincide with linear functors having the property that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊕
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\otimes = F_\oplus
            as functors (so that the same functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is
            = succeed "-lax" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            = succeed "-colax" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊕
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus
            = succeed "," do
