          %p
            Conversely, a polycategory that is suitably “representable” yields a linearly distributive category. Also, the forgetful functor from linearly distributive categories and
            %em strong Frobenius
            linear functors to polycategories has a left adjoint (
            %a{:href => "#CockettSeely97"}> Cockett-Seely 1997
            ).
          %h3#symmetric_monoidal_categories Symmetric monoidal categories
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            becomes a linearly distributive category in which both monoidal structures are the same; all the required structure and coherence conditions follow straight from coherence for symmetric monoidal categories. Note that such examples are almost never
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous"} star-autonomous
          %p
            These examples satisfy the extra property that the distributors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi B
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \oplus B \coloneqq A \otimes \bot^{-1} \otimes B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            is a chosen
            = succeed "-invertible" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
          %h3#linear_bicategories Linear bicategories
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+bicategory"} Linear bicategories
            are a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizontal+categorification"} horizontal categorification
            of linearly distributive categories (
            %a{:href => "#CKS99"}> Cockett-Koslowski-Seely 1999
            ).
          %h2#internal_structures Internal structures
          %p
            Some structures normally defined in a monoidal category can more generally be defined in a linearly distributive category. This includes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+objects"} dual objects
            (yielding the above “negations”) and also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+algebras"} Frobenius algebras
            (
            %a{:href => "#Egger2010"}> Egger 2010
            ).
          %h2#as_lax_frobenius_algebras As lax Frobenius algebras
          %p
            Linearly distributive categories that are both closed and co-closed can be identified with lax
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+algebras"} Frobenius algebras
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polycategory"} polycategory
            %span.newWikiWord
              MVar
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/MVar"} ?
            of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multivariable+adjunctions"} multivariable adjunctions
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2017/11/starautonomous_categories_are.html"} this blog post
          %h2#history History
          %p
            A key feature of linearly distributive categories is the existence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\delta \colon A \otimes (B \oplus C) \longrightarrow (A \otimes B) \oplus C
          %p
            which exhibit linear
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributivity"} distributivity
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
            %a{:href => "#CockettSeely97"} Cockett-Seely 1997
            referred to
            %a{:href => "#Lambek93"} Lambek 1993
            for a survey of some of those developments.
          %p
            This concept was called a
            %em weakly distributive category
            when it was presented by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robin+Cockett"} Robin Cockett
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Seely"} Robert Seely
            at the 1991 Durham LMS symposium on applications of categories in computer science (
            %a{:href => "#CockettSeely92"} Cockett-Seely 1992
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#CockettSeely97"} Cockett-Seely 1997
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valeria+de+Paiva"} Valeria de Paiva
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hyland"} Martin Hyland
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi GC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{GC}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Yves+Girard"} Jean-Yves Girard
            at a 1987 Boulder conference for inspiring the construction. (
            %a{:href => "#dePaiva91"}> de Paiva 1991
            )
          %p
            Subsequently, in recognition of the concept’s fundamental role in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+theory"} proof theory
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+categories"} distributive categories
            in any standard sense, these categories were called
            = succeed "." do
              %em linearly distributive categories
          %p
            Linearly distributive categories allowed the modularization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+theory"} proof theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            and a more systematic approach to the then unsolved
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence"} coherence
            problems associated with the units in linear logic. This program of work culminated in a solution which was published in (
            %a{:href => "#BCST"}> BCST 1996
            )
          %p
            These techniques also solved a classical open problem in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+categories"} closed monoidal categories
            concerning the behaviour of the triple dual.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"} Todd Trimble
          %p
            Linearly distributive categories are to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polycategories"} polycategories
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            are to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategories"} multicategories
          %p
            Contrary to the general direction of work in coherence (and, indeed, in the linear logic community), the solution to the problem, which was given in (
            %a{:href => "#BCST"}> BCST 1996
            ), involved an expansion reduction rewriting system modulo equations. At that time, the prevailing proof theoretic strategy for solving coherence problems was simply to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cut+elimination"} cut-eliminate
          %p
            At that time this was generally thought to be a rather unsatisfactory and unnecessarily technical solution especially when compared to the normal forms to which the community was accustomed. However, in 2014, Willem Heijltjes and Robin Houston (
            %a{:href => "#HH"}> HH2014
            ) showed that equality of morphism is a
            = succeed "-complete" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                PSPACE
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/PSPACE"} ?
          %p Linearly distributive categories have now been used as the substrate for a number of systems.
